# splice `system_prompt_mode = "strip"` pattern list — Claude Code's operating prompt WITHOUT the
# hedge paragraphs (V4-170, operator ask 2026-09-19). One regex per line; a paragraph of the
# client's system text that any line matches is deleted at the wire; every other paragraph rides
# through untouched. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Captured from Claude Code 2.1.276 on 2026-09-19 (both the interactive and the -p prompt): each
# pattern names its paragraph by its opening words, so a wording change inside the paragraph does
# not unhook it. Re-capture on a Claude Code upgrade: point a throwaway session at a request
# recorder (ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL) and diff its `system` blocks against these.
#
# Use it from a project-head layer, so only that project on that head loses the paragraphs:
#   [projects."~/work/pokemon-bot".heads.bonsai]
#   system_prompt_mode = "strip"
#   system_prompt_file = "~/.config/splice/prompts/claude-code-hedges.strip"

# The security-refusal paragraph: a small local model reads it as a refusal instruction and
# refuses ordinary work that merely sounds like it (bots, scrapers, credentials in a config).
^IMPORTANT: Assist with authorized security testing

# The confirm-first paragraph: "For actions that are hard to reverse or outward-facing, confirm
# first" — the hedge a head driven under an operator's own standing authorization does not want.
^For actions that are hard to reverse or outward-facing, confirm first
